e "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358387} {:answer/id #uuid "604bb718-8188-5706-9d5e-bc5e825254c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358594} {:answer/id #uuid "64729ba5-db61-5904-a7f3-3348bf688c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358631} {:answer/id #uuid "95c21078-c3b5-5a29-a41b-fa3dd5c92b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359063} {:answer/id #uuid "9903b498-b87b-528c-9348-0e7f32f90a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95c21078-c3b5-5a29-a41b-fa3dd5c92b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4becacdb-3199-5e03-8693-24305826113a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360114}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21e5af7d-c901-55dc-94b7-2ce8f4a4e5f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96663" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30abb0c4-1bff-50f9-9fca-c00eba3cf9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422285} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe184a0-615a-5d44-a917-af918964b500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe184a0-615a-5d44-a917-af918964b500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422286} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba99938-250e-5ae5-bf0d-5fb8ffd0e043" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a407fa-f792-5416-9af8-1c23d1ca46fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422287} {:answer/id #uuid "d6eee552-87f0-587e-9972-7d671e2c86b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6eee552-87f0-587e-9972-7d671e2c86b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07ca297-62a7-5e03-8fea-27d8baa6b5a5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed3c95ff-45e3-5a8f-97c1-8b53553ad9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422289} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae2557d-e9db-57cc-b828-db6340b0d291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed3c95ff-45e3-5a8f-97c1-8b53553ad9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422289} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb037b0-ed04-5067-99cf-fa5cc12a74f1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422425}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1054102-ae5b-5062-8507-4d26889215d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96672" :question/problem "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}{x}^{3}-2y{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n- **II.** $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2y=2{y}^{′}$\n- **III.** $4{y}^{‴}x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166652 :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06525551-8771-58aa-abe0-a9f198bc1187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422261} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf9b44-e10e-530c-98cd-737312ed32b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf9b44-e10e-530c-98cd-737312ed32b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422262} :answer-field/id #uuid "13241252-12fa-5fc2-a7aa-1c53e17cb337" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7ee4e9-11d0-5f10-a3df-a90cf4050692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422263} {:answer/id #uuid "64afbc79-5ad4-597f-bed9-2806cd57a48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64afbc79-5ad4-597f-bed9-2806cd57a48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422264} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0f2bfd-a2c5-5edc-b594-edd014ea404a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33fba92-6be7-5fec-96aa-554a753fbfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422265} {:answer/id #uuid "89e83999-951c-5d6a-b8b5-25333fd7dc61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33fba92-6be7-5fec-96aa-554a753fbfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3da8c301-99da-5e5e-8435-46dc10bd41c5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- **III.** $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac40970-e6e7-5ee8-ac6c-1fac307ec4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422273} {:answer/id #uuid "69bc2ad9-2521-5c14-b8db-bc8a36f5b9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69bc2ad9-2521-5c14-b8db-bc8a36f5b9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422274} :answer-field/id #uuid "c227025b-4dfb-5ce9-821e-510b71e1cdfc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422417} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e18e0e7-caac-5bdf-ac4b-225b115fa69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422275} {:answer/id #uuid "0b188ba2-6c75-5d2c-925d-6863beb94c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e18e0e7-caac-5bdf-ac4b-225b115fa69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422275} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dba96b9-06a1-5a54-b796-7d298384987b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3384518d-7a79-55c7-9801-cb13d141faa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422277} {:answer/id #uuid "ce709378-3669-57f3-9116-a908fe234d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce709378-3669-57f3-9116-a908fe234d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422278} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d10848-dfc0-5e56-9cf1-aa09031f2696" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422419}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ca3e21f-6e14-592d-bc53-233d6a2ad52a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96387" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- **III.** $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad13619-d7b5-5d2f-b25b-0ed6aa30151f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422231} {:answer/id #uuid "caaf15ce-b06c-57ed-abf8-2ab34f65a039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caaf15ce-b06c-57ed-abf8-2ab34f65a039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422232} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a8174b-4d8d-5b7d-a5ff-4c39764dc726" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760c1112-d0a9-5b72-84d1-6232c1e23202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422233} {:answer/id #uuid "af5af3e2-59d5-55dd-9167-d6287704596f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5af3e2-59d5-55dd-9167-d6287704596f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422234} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1040e7e-ad47-5805-b4ad-f5639fd421cb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d177fa-8dbe-5db2-8b85-40d491d016f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422235} {:answer/id #uuid "115c9ae8-f49c-5c69-9889-f210b446b628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12d177fa-8dbe-5db2-8b85-40d491d016f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422235} :answer-field/id #uuid "073faff4-fa7e-5885-b9cf-d9a41f7de5bb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98fb69b1-0fe2-5868-8c3d-537e50a23e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-118863" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\n- **III.** $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ca1a41-55a5-59ad-9001-0e2459c8bb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422297} {:answer/id #uuid "e4011267-674e-5cd5-a96a-edbdb7f51458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ca1a41-55a5-59ad-9001-0e2459c8bb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422297} :answer-field/id #uuid "33de995d-3b57-550c-9c7d-aa966ecdaf48" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73cc998-84c0-5fc9-b67a-7c22e493f437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422299} {:answer/id #uuid "a43ee464-225b-5bac-b829-4893ccf7ec82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a43ee464-225b-5bac-b829-4893ccf7ec82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422300} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f86d8f-f572-5fe3-9a19-500e72f3ef0c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf0c55d7-5ac5-57f8-97e1-27c64bfea087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422301} {:answer/id #uuid "03394c62-f0fb-510f-ad01-72010e84b8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf0c55d7-5ac5-57f8-97e1-27c64bfea087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422301} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6038821-1293-59ab-9ec7-324905ffa46c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb765ab-56a4-5072-853c-55e0a9cdf71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96702" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x={e}^{y}$\n- **II.** $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2=\\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y}=5y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{y}+x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={e}^{y}+x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232eaa66-0237-5dd3-a69f-3e3272efb858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422303} {:answer/id #uuid "11d01465-58b4-5e71-ad95-2ed8f89b7195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232eaa66-0237-5dd3-a69f-3e3272efb858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422303} :answer-field/id #uuid "da09c621-2ab3-5008-9fd4-8147a7d93986" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91da46cd-4fec-5066-ad81-e05c51f102c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422305} {:answer/id #uuid "73818976-acff-57a9-95f9-347242cb9a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73818976-acff-57a9-95f9-347242cb9a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422306} :answer-field/id #uuid "dceb4f87-957a-593d-8d26-547fd90cf20a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d54ec6f4-81ae-505d-a857-5706b473527e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422307} {:answer/id #uuid "f81d0c2e-9ec1-5b79-942c-6f32380ff6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f81d0c2e-9ec1-5b79-942c-6f32380ff6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422308} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0c1e47-f563-527f-b385-60bdc727eba0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ca7da4a-7115-52ea-963d-df4449aa6da2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96703" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=2$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488ffaa-acc4-55b1-84c6-7107a1c151cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422309} {:answer/id #uuid "c67a1cca-c082-592e-8ecb-e3117b7bb71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67a1cca-c082-592e-8ecb-e3117b7bb71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422310} :answer-field/id #uuid "064f00e8-e3b6-52e0-8529-c9f0f14fd0ce" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50501def-4ff1-5a98-9e69-d4d1e9355ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422311} {:answer/id #uuid "b69d5df5-08af-5c32-991c-9c15c44dd068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50501def-4ff1-5a98-9e69-d4d1e9355ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422311} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cdca183-2209-5d53-aefc-42d46c84ca21" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb6948d0-acc3-5286-8891-af6b60d2e31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422313} {:answer/id #uuid "93a8855e-cb56-5720-b143-7887ce875bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6948d0-acc3-5286-8891-af6b60d2e31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422313} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fedbad3-4114-58f9-b8c9-fe8ec7b61ebd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "483de092-6fe5-5b9f-85ea-10c713e1d18a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96705" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- **III.** $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486cbf20-6ed1-5ea6-919b-5e3452e6e684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422321} {:answer/id #uuid "aafcaf30-4b85-5f57-a825-f8813f70bba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "486cbf20-6ed1-5ea6-919b-5e3452e6e684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422321} :answer-field/id #uuid "466ce07a-3407-5b72-8d1c-b0b7154f1088" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5bfc78a-3e28-567c-8ff8-90cd62f225d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422323} {:answer/id #uuid "c6099c92-190c-5449-992e-da3da0f24f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5bfc78a-3e28-567c-8ff8-90cd62f225d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422323} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba0cf42-3e95-5a7b-8ea1-38a1d871a8d9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6bf198-bac2-537d-ac1b-b42144e1064c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422325} {:answer/id #uuid "084202a6-2162-54d6-9598-13a0685564ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084202a6-2162-54d6-9598-13a0685564ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422326} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bad82c9-2c1b-5905-9265-70409b24568a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422443}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad324671-5207-59c5-b2d9-048cb88806c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-96712" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{'}=1-\\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y={e}^{2}$\n- **III.** $x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y}=4$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{4y+1}{xy} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y+1}{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429ab1e6-1ea8-591f-8018-488e0328c633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422219} {:answer/id #uuid "8abbe2b3-d552-5095-beac-5364c9bb1f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8abbe2b3-d552-5095-beac-5364c9bb1f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422220} :answer-field/id #uuid "932c7315-fbec-5137-99ad-8b3d6abcc576" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae25728a-975e-5157-92a8-8d1a96f74031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422221} {:answer/id #uuid "8dabfa11-04b9-5367-929d-f3c2edf37075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae25728a-975e-5157-92a8-8d1a96f74031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422221} :answer-field/id #uuid "29377ff2-9a08-5ae1-b11b-c0869a6af61d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d642f39-1ba7-582e-99be-e5783acf5bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422223} {:answer/id #uuid "a488d4a0-572b-5e44-a907-0c207d0eb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a488d4a0-572b-5e44-a907-0c207d0eb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422224} :answer-field/id #uuid "674b78a9-e972-5af8-91cd-bcf759582826" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422392}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4fb73e0-2435-50c4-b136-c8ed7ccb224f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118858" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c2cd6-04ab-56ea-9090-bfc8e049d542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422225} {:answer/id #uuid "78ae71d2-619b-56f3-92bf-ecfeebf9c3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "205c2cd6-04ab-56ea-9090-bfc8e049d542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422225} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adb79a2-2fca-5fcf-8dd7-6acc5f68f3a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e49df7-3c70-5cd8-beb4-ff959fd404aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422227} {:answer/id #uuid "38051905-c461-5911-8df5-70c9f959f1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38051905-c461-5911-8df5-70c9f959f1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422228} :answer-field/id #uuid "cacddabc-75dd-59ce-afe3-05d7d1ba8798" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a5168fb-5c6b-5a1a-8194-633d3b1f1dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422229} {:answer/id #uuid "f7168c22-e701-5df5-af8a-614a1a97b22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a5168fb-5c6b-5a1a-8194-633d3b1f1dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422229} :answer-field/id #uuid "944c44c1-dc25-512a-858d-9642a5fec880" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81da2b48-aa38-5eb5-8b7d-567a07263315" :question/math-academy-id "q-118859" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2888360-9f66-5c23-981b-f079328c8636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422237} {:answer/id #uuid "d327d689-8c39-594c-96b9-58a570c5b701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2888360-9f66-5c23-981b-f079328c8636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c873029d-589f-57a0-a4d0-8437caa76bc0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b6cd63-9458-513b-b2aa-d028666ac3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422239} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf41c95-b46b-5d57-a7e0-be741ba1e4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73b6cd63-9458-513b-b2aa-d028666ac3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422239} :answer-field/id #uuid "d843cc15-36f5-5f93-b122-c8351e2ae5d6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa6421fe-c55f-5658-bf62-c0dde5e44e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422241} {:answer/id #uuid "9a24fdb8-a81b-5920-a551-f1e49499aba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a24fdb8-a81b-5920-a551-f1e49499aba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422242} :answer-field/id #uuid "d809c0ac-7d83-52cd-b318-f7087cf971e1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422401}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40dc1226-9a4e-5dff-a14a-c81909c8002d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118868" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd904cf8-8e31-5dfb-a522-bc91b00cd7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422243} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6f67a5-b49f-5fbd-8497-f7c6a4031fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd904cf8-8e31-5dfb-a522-bc91b00cd7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422243} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd43700f-31c2-5c61-b73c-b13e37eff052" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422402} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d289e9c-3443-581d-a43b-b8699cb35799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422245} {:answer/id #uuid "27ec5b50-7d97-5078-8643-a6c4784da9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ec5b50-7d97-5078-8643-a6c4784da9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422246} :answer-field/id #uuid "12514015-bd25-5ca0-9c7a-27d17e773778" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6565a714-41fd-50da-aa58-7c4bb033f40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422247} {:answer/id #uuid "c6441d94-73d1-5d09-a421-cb127ecdc684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6441d94-73d1-5d09-a421-cb127ecdc684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422248} :answer-field/id #uuid "3304dbae-d540-59d7-9271-398328295560" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1c949f8-b9ab-5207-8749-bf7052ce14f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-118869" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1ea97b-c130-5985-8c17-cc72347ebe97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422249} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2223d5-e7e4-52cb-b31c-fde19140dc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1ea97b-c130-5985-8c17-cc72347ebe97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422249} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe17e70-19b7-564c-8b0d-d7279deacc98" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4a5bf0-ec42-5cdd-9f4a-eafa46fc3dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422251} {:answer/id #uuid "70a78532-3820-5a35-b1c4-80a7950ecbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4a5bf0-ec42-5cdd-9f4a-eafa46fc3dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422251} :answer-field/id #uuid "006b96f3-1130-5007-b040-c8341e58057b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4e37f4d-fa5b-5fe7-96e5-e9ef0b93d247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422253} {:answer/id #uuid "73afeb6e-bb5d-5e60-a750-aabc89815638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73afeb6e-bb5d-5e60-a750-aabc89815638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422254} :answer-field/id #uuid "c68d0e4a-42ad-5fc6-a993-d34e08e87259" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e075483-5dc5-5bbb-be2d-e2d1164eea57" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c8c0cda-78bf-55f7-88e5-b25b198bc750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422315} {:answer/id #uuid "02410bef-7524-5134-a4f8-ebab62b11469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02410bef-7524-5134-a4f8-ebab62b11469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422316} :answer-field/id #uuid "264fc7d0-805e-5bdb-83e7-23bd58448ad6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447cd238-ce2a-5aa7-aa7b-1cd804b8d54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422317} {:answer/id #uuid "a248c664-9709-57ad-a3ee-d65103a93863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a248c664-9709-57ad-a3ee-d65103a93863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422318} :answer-field/id #uuid "661ef71b-f6a7-5216-a51b-7056fd0d53e9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdd71bd-d2a4-550e-bfe3-41eb169f5bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422319} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef1a9b6-6b2d-597b-9ef5-e76a8a9c1843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abdd71bd-d2a4-550e-bfe3-41eb169f5bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422319} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa5233b-2a0d-5987-9db1-ae437c736ff9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422440}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5388ae42-2792-5587-8853-f61a559c11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b017fb70-0c5d-548a-9f29-f8c54647f96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422327} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c9333b-f353-5073-a8ae-7603ecd03ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b017fb70-0c5d-548a-9f29-f8c54647f96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422327} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6f0560-1c9e-5df2-9134-4ff3ff9b0077" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05aaa1e5-c77a-5c69-9336-ba40efd3dca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422329} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc4734c-e2bd-5c34-bcd3-d7f463c81508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc4734c-e2bd-5c34-bcd3-d7f463c81508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422330} :answer-field/id #uuid "a638c313-64c2-5397-bf14-121c910b5d46" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422445} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82717268-aeb7-59c7-9ead-ed0bccb337be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422331} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4573cc-8220-585b-b628-542222050b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82717268-aeb7-59c7-9ead-ed0bccb337be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422331} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec4389f-6741-54f0-af32-ea647439c22b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83ac0667-a1bc-5ca7-903e-f359054c0435" :question/math-academy-id "q-96713" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{-y}{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=2{e}^{x}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}{y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04341a8f-3a94-579e-8ed0-431601950988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422333} {:answer/id #uuid "933327bb-2dcb-55ed-9335-b26ba314554c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "933327bb-2dcb-55ed-9335-b26ba314554c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422334} :answer-field/id #uuid "9936342f-6870-5e95-ba09-39e58aa96ef6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8d5d5c0-2cef-5399-a6c1-f746b7828321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422335} {:answer/id #uuid "93b31d93-8632-56ab-bde4-e4b2c3167c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8d5d5c0-2cef-5399-a6c1-f746b7828321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422335} :answer-field/id #uuid "031eea6f-b2ad-5dad-81de-3aaca36f0c13" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d505e7-6ead-59e7-afc6-c53e477668cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422337} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4cf2d2-b3e1-544c-bdc2-60420b6c2957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d505e7-6ead-59e7-afc6-c53e477668cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422337} :answer-field/id #uuid "e894eb15-9d72-5d21-b95b-aa045a59f3ab" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb1a3b53-c6cd-51f3-bcd2-63da1f7e9756" :question/math-academy-id "q-96715" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166653 :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3c3a223-c2a8-5949-b84f-fa769ba4b994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422267} {:answer/id #uuid "2a84d494-2aef-50d3-8cf8-8db249714a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a84d494-2aef-50d3-8cf8-8db249714a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422268} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc2e313-626b-537a-8e9d-2a960f01b586" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc5c8df0-0441-5501-a1ad-fe7911ffce95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422269} {:answer/id #uuid "077d77c6-30fd-55f9-a1d3-9a54c78855cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc5c8df0-0441-5501-a1ad-fe7911ffce95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422269} :answer-field/id #uuid "a974ae1f-fc72-5264-8a9d-0c00c4c5d83e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64826bbf-2236-5228-a180-82aed0ae287a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422271} {:answer/id #uuid "b08b77cf-69a9-541b-947c-c12cefd2115b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64826bbf-2236-5228-a180-82aed0ae287a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422271} :answer-field/id #uuid "72555dfe-0212-54c7-b6a2-17c4d03e6ad3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c01fd7ca-ed83-5e17-8036-cd03a49c52f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95985" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- **III.** $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1657ec-ee94-56ab-9624-eb98b93a3f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422339} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba78363-9c7b-5138-bdae-fcc1c1e8b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1657ec-ee94-56ab-9624-eb98b93a3f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422339} :answer-field/id #uuid "91464ac2-012a-5628-9de2-8468b2c1d708" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3baf4f4e-994c-5a3d-8cd7-a08d6815e9e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422341} {:answer/id #uuid "ca77a9c5-67ca-5442-a2b6-728548b85c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca77a9c5-67ca-5442-a2b6-728548b85c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422342} :answer-field/id #uuid "df06a3a0-7e3c-5b74-b120-cc16d360e1b5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9b50315-f8d9-5f53-a15e-c35152183cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422343} {:answer/id #uuid "00362934-c6f7-5519-a9c5-57cedf485a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b50315-f8d9-5f53-a15e-c35152183cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422343} :answer-field/id #uuid "41909812-23d6-5023-aa6c-ce2d4a23c2b5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422452}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- **II.** $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- **III.** $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853427ec-ff39-510f-b5b9-b9b3063c4fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422189} {:answer/id #uuid "26f5c7b4-02a7-523d-971a-7bb512830680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26f5c7b4-02a7-523d-971a-7bb512830680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422190} :answer-field/id #uuid "f073f5b0-a749-5824-acf4-fa526c4e38fd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2303430c-0704-550c-b6ae-374bc966f21d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422191} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a09a9d-3bac-5120-8742-15c47fbf484c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2303430c-0704-550c-b6ae-374bc966f21d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422191} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f00add-7f80-5655-aba8-bf0b7b031d57" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e36a2e5-c84a-5bb7-86bc-439a8b342c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422193} {:answer/id #uuid "707a477f-c377-547a-845c-170d00c19430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e36a2e5-c84a-5bb7-86bc-439a8b342c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422193} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13181d5-74b1-59ab-977c-f7a710603e44" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422377}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7775ce93-f97a-564e-ab19-6ca38ffa6f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118786" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\n- **II.** $8{y}^{′}-xy=3$\n- **III.** $3{y}^{″}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31ed816f-dcc9-55e7-a7ee-b8893ddc6b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422345} {:answer/id #uuid "7c033af8-4e67-5059-a3d7-2ee152e006ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31ed816f-dcc9-55e7-a7ee-b8893ddc6b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422345} :answer-field/id #uuid "25e14789-c319-5f0e-bc5c-74e15f504fc6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "891fce35-cd60-5014-954c-b452492c406b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422347} {:answer/id #uuid "c19a760c-5182-53bb-9e1c-45300e79eda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "891fce35-cd60-5014-954c-b452492c406b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422347} :answer-field/id #uuid "88417ad9-d92d-5c8a-9985-1095d72e3b30" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1015ba8-7c86-5225-bd73-031db89ebe78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422349} {:answer/id #uuid "7b79acea-a0fa-5455-a4fd-773c08b30342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b79acea-a0fa-5455-a4fd-773c08b30342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422350} :answer-field/id #uuid "a349157e-eeba-5e25-b578-93131466b86c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85607d47-df50-5e10-bf9d-699a7a42f32f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96987" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3$\n- **II.** $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,\\,{y}^{''}(0)=2$\n- **III.** $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y},\\,y(0)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,$ and ${y}^{''}(0)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8efe158e-5199-5887-8b3f-0d56b86243f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422363} {:answer/id #uuid "802b1980-87f7-5e28-9c43-ca80dce3ab91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "802b1980-87f7-5e28-9c43-ca80dce3ab91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422364} :answer-field/id #uuid "48575eb9-08b1-5ad9-be32-59c64f199db4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422462} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3fe1fd-a8ce-5d7b-bd4f-b4fbe96c661f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422365} {:answer/id #uuid "82cb1afb-17dc-54bc-9444-9e13f04751f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cb1afb-17dc-54bc-9444-9e13f04751f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422366} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bffdb09-ada5-51cd-a74f-e8bfbb314c3e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a05862-f216-5d32-b34b-f327419d2f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422367} {:answer/id #uuid "f8675194-82ce-500b-a188-7d2c2088e9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82a05862-f216-5d32-b34b-f327419d2f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422367} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d5434f-556d-5bac-b0c3-5f61c1206ea5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e83e1ae-37f6-5806-9569-e87bd10cd10f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96992" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc37352-81e0-514c-bedf-fa91579d6398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422195} {:answer/id #uuid "00fbec5e-2569-5e70-887a-76fd721447ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fbec5e-2569-5e70-887a-76fd721447ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422196} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d2c7ba3-872a-5747-84b2-d758a496b0c6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d471821-5101-5272-a1e9-64c5df191a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422197} {:answer/id #uuid "e79714fa-47cd-53d1-87a1-cb84a5b63e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d471821-5101-5272-a1e9-64c5df191a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422197} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f72266-3ebb-5e6f-a355-a91fc75722dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d77c166-99a6-5b75-9890-9bbf922be8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422199} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d66aee-5929-509b-ad21-8bd403ae74fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d77c166-99a6-5b75-9890-9bbf922be8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0ca13e-0a9c-5b4c-8a39-481f747b601a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422380}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4247b2af-dd08-543e-82b1-e9616731f453" :question/math-academy-id "q-118787" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- **III.** $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289bb35b-99fe-5106-99b0-f71c04163a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422201} {:answer/id #uuid "2302fbb6-0adc-5661-bbf7-af76484dacc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "289bb35b-99fe-5106-99b0-f71c04163a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422201} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e48c6aa-29b3-5af2-98e8-cf9155d9f86e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beef713f-b9cc-55dc-8f1d-6cf37ec09f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422203} {:answer/id #uuid "e499fae0-7b48-5f3b-93bb-81ab64c9da66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e499fae0-7b48-5f3b-93bb-81ab64c9da66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422204} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbae87b9-4781-5997-879b-2dc1d3b6b122" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aac407af-d819-550f-805d-c2f156435fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422205} {:answer/id #uuid "983242a4-a136-5392-8243-ada487eed7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aac407af-d819-550f-805d-c2f156435fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422205} :answer-field/id #uuid "259806e0-5727-5f05-a402-006bfaa14dbc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422383}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "941e9c2c-1b18-5669-bb2b-964e53cd46ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-118788" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- **II.** $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- **III.** $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d275665e-21d6-5021-9af4-963d5728df52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422207} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b7cd67-3643-54bb-814b-c0117b35907e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b7cd67-3643-54bb-814b-c0117b35907e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422208} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdfa9c1-4f01-5411-a857-7dd9685e500c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9417f602-3568-5efe-94c7-501d5cff75d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422209} {:answer/id #uuid "86b7ef39-bf9a-54c6-a085-f31d633793e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86b7ef39-bf9a-54c6-a085-f31d633793e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422210} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef92c25e-8c54-5dcb-95f2-841eb93872dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89bb1fe1-b3be-5274-ac26-302e27a47abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422211} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab84d7e-d700-5891-a5c2-71baae4994b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89bb1fe1-b3be-5274-ac26-302e27a47abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422211} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a7fdd2-30a3-5167-9a37-4b4c0e0e01ad" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422386}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c7ca5ad-bcbb-5515-be28-446ac5363a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-118790" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+y=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5caf84-b369-53c1-92d8-3daf6511f002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422213} {:answer/id #uuid "d814dbc8-353f-574a-8589-9891a880a534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e5caf84-b369-53c1-92d8-3daf6511f002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422213} :answer-field/id #uuid "66d4f45e-d07b-5e2f-a6dc-8a5dc04ecd85" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fccfea10-d196-5af8-881d-6efaf713593e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422215} {:answer/id #uuid "26116a3c-17a4-5e07-abc8-396b55612f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26116a3c-17a4-5e07-abc8-396b55612f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1b9058-f874-55f5-b4b3-7338f7856cb8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10764352-ef44-5719-87cb-3b2d8938735f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422217} {:answer/id #uuid "e37e4e1a-2748-569f-9af1-4faf43505566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37e4e1a-2748-569f-9af1-4faf43505566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422218} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcab1241-b9e2-53d5-94b4-15742cef1c28" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422389}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7204e641-7358-50b0-b9c2-d492a160bc98" :question/math-academy-id "q-118791" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- **III.** $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d1ca0c-c077-564f-bbd3-6bd589f331e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422351} {:answer/id #uuid "87ce1d0c-32be-5143-a872-7d49dc66b488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d1ca0c-c077-564f-bbd3-6bd589f331e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422351} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fca4796-70aa-50d1-96e1-5e660ddb7451" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5727dd59-2f56-5c06-9b43-b63ff7c2bfb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422353} {:answer/id #uuid "842ba921-5998-5c95-bb52-17e348e3161e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "842ba921-5998-5c95-bb52-17e348e3161e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422354} :answer-field/id #uuid "658f8908-07e3-5b79-8798-c03791ed181b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8203d5-1002-51cb-9411-d4a83fc42ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422355} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4c0de1-0610-56fb-9005-f107985bbfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b8203d5-1002-51cb-9411-d4a83fc42ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422355} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff5351b-54cc-5ac7-889b-b6d3ef3ffe59" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765ad5be-eea7-5159-b982-461fb59d4587" :question/math-academy-id "q-96988" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- **II.** $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- **III.** $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc873da1-dcb5-58cd-bcdc-f6dfaecd44eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422357} {:answer/id #uuid "aabe49eb-f60e-59a4-8e8e-b51a643069e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aabe49eb-f60e-59a4-8e8e-b51a643069e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422358} :answer-field/id #uuid "793806ab-841f-56f5-aae9-1f5aa1b28e93" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e61410e-bb7d-5fd5-a42c-93728f72a519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422359} {:answer/id #uuid "3904d9fc-81e9-58dc-b100-87ee0a13dfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e61410e-bb7d-5fd5-a42c-93728f72a519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422359} :answer-field/id #uuid "503ea99b-8961-55e4-a7e1-ce13e2130792" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4347d9eb-3074-5e4f-bb59-94bee475caab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422361} {:answer/id #uuid "82b802ce-94eb-52a4-a016-7da0aee33a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82b802ce-94eb-52a4-a016-7da0aee33a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422362} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f981e7a-b93e-545d-9956-04d8aae8a018" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422461}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a63b835-35e3-55e6-8b41-bbb1ab5d0792" :question/math-academy-id "q-96990" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(1)=1$\n- **II.** $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4},\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=1$\n- **III.** $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y,\\,y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{‴}-x{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{4}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=1.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{3}{y}^{′}-3x=2y$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166696 :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b69b16c0-aa13-5778-b2ef-bc583efe6b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y + 1)e^{xy}i + xe^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163373} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3d328d-a7a7-526c-bbd9-76e4717393d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + x^{2}e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163374} {:answer/id #uuid "c78bec72-c383-5d7e-b9a0-8f4e09c5580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xye^{xy}i + (x + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163375} {:answer/id #uuid "d55039d7-cd35-55e4-ad8f-f14b8bdfbbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + (xy + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163376} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f477f-887d-5565-aa72-281d9f2765d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ye^{xy}i + xye^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163377}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa74a3cb-2703-5e63-b05a-f75c6855f983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163372} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad573080-12c0-5795-bd57-186bf4ef86e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386077} {:answer/id #uuid "6637e684-fc51-5e28-9ef7-e989f843fa89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x+1){e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386078} {:answer/id #uuid "bde9cf33-16b6-581e-a442-aa5532c2cea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386079} {:answer/id #uuid "c7adf677-b962-5e7e-8a2d-2c8fa74b77c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1){e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386080} {:answer/id #uuid "8933c795-8d32-5777-bb3b-9769cea078c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}\\,i+xy{e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad573080-12c0-5795-bd57-186bf4ef86e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386077} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e92d39-741b-5c6a-8be4-edf3e06fa576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce34f68f-f91f-5032-a961-dafacf6d59a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104399" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f81d2d-d5c5-56a4-9386-45b306a54421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi + x^{2}zj + (x^{2}y + 2z)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166455} {:answer/id #uuid "114c77c1-eeaa-5774-b9a8-841df8fb3176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}yzi + x^{2}yj - (2xy + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166456} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce24d24-00cc-5dfa-ae57-7b576ed54e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyzi - y^{2}zj + (y^{2}x + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166457} {:answer/id #uuid "3375422a-0420-5431-9e77-b5991e7f6f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi - x^{2}yj + (xz^{2} + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166458} {:answer/id #uuid "3658104d-2b4f-5a06-af82-d41092238392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyzi - x^{2}j + 4z^{2}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166459}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ee6962-d399-5885-8c69-5ff1918c56ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1fa5bbb-0cad-5524-8a00-80b3cc811832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+4{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395571} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e38753-dc36-5e53-ae48-e8e105ddc9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyz\\,i-{y}^{2}z\\,j+({y}^{2}x+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395572} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9c13bd-298a-540b-842f-319d1b0d07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395573} {:answer/id #uuid "00fabcdd-6a3b-5644-9c42-6c00944bb040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j-(2xy+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395574} {:answer/id #uuid "8d53317e-4f51-5b33-8e38-81c2f63e228f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j+(x{z}^{2}+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce9c13bd-298a-540b-842f-319d1b0d07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395573} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c66c02b-c2b6-50bd-9cb2-f45f0ace8800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4d7c82-2e83-562b-b6bc-3ec6c276e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98947" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c79e65f-4657-574d-9b28-b9892ee40b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238293} {:answer/id #uuid "7eceeb7e-4643-589d-8a42-a35eb4275ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i+y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239158} {:answer/id #uuid "8baa56bb-b0ab-5fd3-9d83-4ae38a0f0cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239266} {:answer/id #uuid "a8db757c-0e28-5da6-9dc7-d0eddb5e601d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239527} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cda0b4-4f82-58cb-b44a-ebd0359b68ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5cda0b4-4f82-58cb-b44a-ebd0359b68ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239796} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0577431-9e1f-5827-b110-3279dc00b9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240724}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f3ac565-e5b1-59a8-9f54-0c7e96f05c2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104396" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2x\\,i-j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45db7d37-257e-5741-9767-a2ee72bc303f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238662} {:answer/id #uuid "a026c4f1-65e8-584a-81f8-49d09aed3845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239453} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3803d-0406-5f4c-b4f7-02086d600d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239500} {:answer/id #uuid "b49e4fa1-e304-508a-b582-63a15e87c038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239636} {:answer/id #uuid "c17f4964-aa3f-54ce-b990-6a3cc4bcb36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3803d-0406-5f4c-b4f7-02086d600d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239500} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dcac30f-063e-5f3e-b8eb-a855b3db8035" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "139d3292-e475-5138-868b-b4cb7235237f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104397" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271a1e1f-1ff6-50b3-b5d4-c1267966fd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238393} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf4b57e-a5d8-5197-87f7-4d37497177cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238584} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcb3d5f-1df2-530a-a137-6eb6c29edfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592